        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-409",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWE decompression bomb (zip bomb): jose/jwe.py:453 calls zlib.decompress(compressed) with NO max_length argument and no post-decompression size check. The attacker fully controls both the trigger and the payload via the JWE token passed to the jwe.decrypt() entry point: _jwe_compact_deserialize(jwe_str) (jwe.py:79) parses the raw token, header is json.loads()'d from the base64url header segment (jwe.py:276->300), and decrypt() then calls _decompress(header.get(\"zip\"), plain_text) at jwe.py:187. The only guards in _decompress are name checks on the zip algorithm -- 'zip not in ZIPS.SUPPORTED' (line 448), 'zip is None' (450), 'zip == ZIPS.DEF' (452) -- none of which bound the output size. An attacker sets the protected header to {\"alg\":\"RSA-OAEP\",\"enc\":\"A256GCM\",\"zip\":\"DEF\"} and supplies a few KB of highly-compressible deflate data that expands to multiple GB, exhausting memory/CPU in the decrypting process. Because RSA-OAEP / RSA1_5 / ECDH-ES are PUBLIC-key encryption, any unauthenticated party holding the recipient's public key can mint a valid token, so this needs no shared secret. Fix: pass a bounded max_length to zlib.decompress (or use a decompressobj with an output cap) and reject oversized output.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived and confirmed. jose/jwe.py:453 calls zlib.decompress(compressed) with no max_length and no post-decompression size check; a tree-wide grep for max_length|decompressobj|MAX_|len(decompressed finds no bound anywhere in the repo (only an unrelated _MAX_RECOVERY_ATTEMPTS in rsa_backend.py:52), so the mitigation is genuinely absent. The attacker controls both trigger and payload: public API jose.jwe.decrypt (jwe.py:60) parses the raw token via _jwe_compact_deserialize (79), base64url-decodes the header segment (276) and json.loads() it (300) with no validation of 'zip' at parse time, then calls _decompress(header.get(\"zip\"), plain_text) at line 187. The only guards in _decompress are name checks -- 'zip not in ZIPS.SUPPORTED' (448), 'zip is None' (450), 'zip == ZIPS.DEF' (452) -- and ZIPS.SUPPORTED = {DEF, None} (constants.py:93-95), so \"zip\":\"DEF\" passes freely; none bound output size. Decompression occurs after _decrypt_and_auth (178), but that does not require a shared secret: ALGORITHMS.SUPPORTED includes RSA_KW = {RSA1_5, RSA-OAEP, RSA-OAEP-256} (constants.py:54,70), which are public-key key-encryption modes, so any unauthenticated party with the recipient's public key can mint a fully valid JWE whose plaintext is a deflate bomb. A few KB of highly compressible data expands to GBs inside the decrypting process. Version pinned at jose/__init__.py:1 __version__ = \"3.3.0\", with CHANGELOG.md:3 showing \"3.4.0 -- UNPUBLISHED\" and no decompression-limit entry -- i.e. the pre-fix state of CVE-2024-33664 (CWE-409), which was remediated in python-jose 3.4.0 by capping decompressed output. ADVISORY CAVEAT: exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so no Metasploit module could be cited; the CVE mapping is context only and this verdict rests on the source evidence. Correcting the analyst's location: the defect spans the whole unguarded branch (448-456), not line 453 in isolation. Severity MEDIUM -- unauthenticated and trivially triggered, but impact is resource exhaustion/DoS only, with no confidentiality or integrity loss."
          },
