        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-696",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "AES-CBC-HMAC padding oracle -- ciphertext is decrypted and PKCS7-unpadded BEFORE the MAC is verified. In jose/jwe.py:_decrypt_and_auth (method at line 208), line 239 runs 'plaintext = encryption_key.decrypt(cipher_text, iv, aad, auth_tag)' and only AFTERWARDS does line 240 check 'auth_tag != auth_tag_check' -> raise JWEError(\"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\") (line 241). For the A*CBC-HS* encs (selected by 'enc in ALGORITHMS.HMAC_AUTH_TAG', line 230), the backend performs NO authentication at all: jose/backends/cryptography_backend.py CryptographyAESKey.decrypt (method line 490) takes the non-GCM branch at line 505 and runs Cipher(algorithms.AES(...), mode).decryptor() -> decryptor.update(cipher_text) (507) -> decryptor.finalize() (508) -> PKCS7(...).unpadder() (509) -> unpadder.update(padded_plain_text) (510) -> unpadder.finalize() (511). unpadder.finalize() raises on invalid PKCS7 padding, producing an error distinguishable from the later 'Invalid JWE Auth Tag' -- a classic padding oracle. cipher_text, iv and auth_tag are all attacker-controlled, split straight out of the token at jwe.py:273 and base64url-decoded. An attacker who can submit chosen JWE tokens and observe which error/latency results can decrypt ciphertext byte-by-byte without the key. Fix: verify the HMAC over (aad||iv||ciphertext||al) in constant time and abort BEFORE invoking any CBC decryption or unpadding (encrypt-then-MAC ordering, per RFC 7518 5.2.2.2).\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from source. jose/jwe.py:_decrypt_and_auth performs full CBC decryption and PKCS7 unpadding of attacker-controlled ciphertext at line 239 BEFORE comparing the authentication tag at line 240-241, violating RFC 7518 5.2.2.2 (encrypt-then-MAC) \u2014 which the code's own comment at jwe.py:175-176 restates (\"rejecting the input without emitting any decrypted output if the JWE Authentication Tag is incorrect\"). For the A*CBC-HS* encs (constants.py:67 HMAC_AUTH_TAG = {A128CBC-HS256, A192CBC-HS384, A256CBC-HS512}, all in SUPPORTED) the backend performs NO authentication: CryptographyAESKey.decrypt takes the non-GCM else-branch (cryptography_backend.py:504-511) and runs Cipher/decryptor.update/finalize then PKCS7 unpadder.update/finalize; the `tag` arg is referenced only inside the GCM branch (496-503). MODES[A128CBC_HS256]=modes.CBC (line 428) confirms CBC mode. The two failure paths are distinguishable: bad padding -> unpadder.finalize() ValueError wrapped at cryptography_backend.py:514 -> re-wrapped at jwe.py:181-182, str(e) ~ \"Invalid padding bytes.\"; good padding + bad MAC -> jwe.py:241, str(e) = \"Invalid JWE Auth Tag\". Same JWEError type, different message => padding oracle for any caller that surfaces/logs str(e). iv/cipher_text/auth_tag are split straight out of the token at jwe.py:273-275 and base64url-decoded; with alg=dir the CEK is the server's fixed key so an attacker replays a captured encrypted_key and iterates arbitrary (iv, ciphertext) pairs \u2014 the classic oracle setup enabling byte-by-byte plaintext recovery without the key. Reachability confirmed, not assumed: graph shows calls at jwe.py 178/230/232/239/240 all reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, and EntryPoint{kind:\"handler\", exposure:\"reachable\"} -> jose/jwe.py:<module>.decrypt (line 60), the public library API. Two corrections to the analyst: (1) the HMAC IS computed at line 232 in the CBC branch (it is merely compared too late), so the practical oracle is the distinguishable error message rather than a large timing gap; (2) line 240 additionally uses a non-constant-time bytes comparison (CWE-208). Version pinned exactly at python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1); the advisory exploit_search step could NOT be completed (tool returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), and CHANGELOG.md has no padding/auth-tag fix entry, so I make no patched-vs-vulnerable claim from exploit references \u2014 the CONFIRM rests solely on the source and graph evidence above. Rated HIGH not CRITICAL because the ordering violation and unauthenticated unpadding are certain and library-internal, while full plaintext recovery depends on the embedding application exposing the two distinct error strings. Fix: compare the HMAC over (aad||iv||ciphertext||al) with hmac.compare_digest and abort before any CBC decryption or unpadding."
          },
