        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-347",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "RSA signature algorithm confusion in the pure-Python fallback backend: jose/backends/rsa_backend.py:210 (RSAKey.verify, method line 206) calls 'pyrsa.verify(msg, sig, self._prepared_key)' and DISCARDS the return value. python-rsa's verify() returns the name of the hash algorithm it recovered from the PKCS#1 v1.5 DigestInfo structure, and it accepts ANY hash algorithm it supports -- it does not constrain the digest to the one the caller expects. Because this result is never compared against self.hash_alg (the digest implied by the JWS 'alg' header, e.g. RS256 vs RS384 vs RS512), a signature legitimately produced over the same signing input with a different/weaker digest is accepted as valid for the claimed algorithm. Combined with the fact that the JWS 'alg' is read from the attacker-controlled header (jose/jws.py:250 'header.get(\"alg\")', centrality 0.000459) and that jws._verify_signature only restricts it when the caller actually passes a non-None algorithms allow-list ('algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms', jws.py:254), an attacker can present a token whose header claims one RSA algorithm while the signature's embedded DigestInfo names another. Note this backend is only active when the 'cryptography' package is absent. Fix: compare pyrsa.verify's returned hash name against the expected self.hash_alg and reject on mismatch.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. jose/backends/rsa_backend.py:206-213 RSAKey.verify() calls pyrsa.verify(msg, sig, self._prepared_key) at line 210 and throws away the return value, returning a bare True. python-rsa's verify() takes no digest parameter -- it recovers the hash algorithm from the PKCS#1 v1.5 DigestInfo embedded in the signature and returns its name -- so with that name discarded, nothing binds the verified digest to self.hash_alg (set at lines 132-136 from the JWS 'alg'). The asymmetry is self-evident in-repo: line 204 sign() DOES pass the digest (pyrsa.sign(msg, key, self.hash_alg)) while line 210 verify() cannot. Every sibling backend pins the digest explicitly (cryptography_backend.py:331 padding.PKCS1v15(), self.hash_alg(); ecdsa_backend.py:106 hashfunc=self.hash_alg), making rsa_backend the sole outlier. Consequence: RS256/RS384/RS512 are interchangeable at verification, and because rsa's HASH_ASN1 table covers MD5 and SHA-1, the verifier's effective digest strength collapses to MD5 regardless of the declared alg -- a direct RFC 7515 4.1.1 alg-binding violation (CWE-347). Reach confirmed in both source and graph: jws.verify (jws.py:48) -> _verify_signature (249, alg = header.get(\"alg\") at 250, allow-list only enforced when 'algorithms is not None' at 254) -> _sig_matches_keys (205) -> key.verify(signing_input, signature) (210); jwt.decode defaults algorithms=None (jwt.py:55). The sink node is reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. I also corrected the analyst's framing of the precondition: this backend is not an edge case but the DEFAULT install -- setup.cfg:34-37 lists only ecdsa/rsa/pyasn1 in install_requires while 'cryptography' is an optional extra at setup.cfg:43-44, so backends/__init__.py:9-13 falls through to rsa_backend on a plain `pip install python-jose`. Tempering the severity: exploitation is not key-free, since the header is part of the signing input, so an attacker still needs a genuine PKCS#1 v1.5 signature over the exact signing input under some accepted digest -- practical vectors are cross-protocol signature reuse where the same RSA key signs with a weak digest elsewhere, or an MD5/SHA-1 collision against a signing oracle. Hence MEDIUM, not HIGH. No test in tests/algorithms/test_RSA.py or test_RSA_compat.py exercises digest binding. exploit_search returned 'corpus unavailable: not indexed' so no exploit-reference signal; the rsa dependency is unpinned (>=4.0,<5.0,!=4.4,!=4.1.1) so no exact-version patch comparison was possible, but the defect is in this repo's code rather than the dependency."
          },
