        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-327",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "The guard that stops an asymmetric public key from being used as an HMAC shared secret has an INCOMPLETE blocklist: in BOTH HMAC backends `invalid_strings` is exactly [b\"-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----\", b\"-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\", b\"ssh-rsa\"]. The OpenSSH public-key prefixes b\"ecdsa-sha2-nistp256\" / b\"ecdsa-sha2-nistp384\" / b\"ecdsa-sha2-nistp521\" and b\"ssh-ed25519\" are ABSENT, so an OpenSSH-format ECDSA/Ed25519 public key passes the check at native.py:46 / cryptography_backend.py:562 and is accepted as the HMAC secret. Combined with jwk.construct() selecting the key class purely from the algorithm name (jwk.py:76 `key_class = get_key(algorithm)`, jwk.py:79 `key_class(key_data, algorithm)`) and jws._sig_matches_keys calling `jwk.construct(key, alg)` at jws.py:208 with `alg` taken from the attacker-controlled JWS header (jws.py:250 `alg = header.get(\"alg\")`), a verifier configured with a public OpenSSH ECDSA key and an algorithms list containing an HS* entry can be made to HMAC-verify with that public (attacker-known) key as the secret, letting the attacker forge arbitrary tokens.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source. The asymmetric-key guard in BOTH HMAC backends blocks only 4 substrings (-----BEGIN PUBLIC KEY-----, -----BEGIN RSA PUBLIC KEY-----, -----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----, ssh-rsa); a repo-wide grep shows 'ecdsa-sha2' and 'ssh-ed25519' appear NOWHERE in the tree, so an OpenSSH-format ECDSA/Ed25519 public key passes native.py:46 / cryptography_backend.py:562 and is stored as prepared_key (native.py:52) and used directly as the HMAC secret in hmac.new(self.prepared_key, ...) (native.py:66). jwk.construct selects the key class purely from the algorithm name (jwk.py:76 get_key(algorithm) -> HMACKey for any ALGORITHMS.HMAC member at jwk.py:34-35; jwk.py:79 key_class(key_data, algorithm)) and never inspects the key material format. The alg is attacker-controlled: jws.py:250 alg = header.get(\"alg\") feeds jws.py:208 jwk.construct(key, alg). Stronger than the analyst claimed: jwt.decode defaults algorithms=None (jwt.py:55) and passes it through (jwt.py:141), while jws.py:254 reads 'if algorithms is not None and alg not in algorithms' \u2014 so the allow-list check is skipped entirely and NO HS* entry is required; jwt.decode(token, openssh_ec_pubkey) honours alg:HS256 from the header, letting an attacker who knows the public key HMAC-sign arbitrary tokens that verify. Graph (scan-scoped) reports reachable_from_entry=true for native.py:39/46, cryptography_backend.py:555/562, jwk.py:76/79, jws.py:208/250, so no unreachability discount applies. Version pinned to 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1) = CVE-2024-33663 (python-jose through 3.3.0, algorithm confusion with OpenSSH ECDSA keys). The guard's existence plus tests/algorithms/test_HMAC.py:15-30 asserting only the 4 known strings proves blocking asymmetric public keys was the intent, so the omission is a defect. Advisory exploit lookup could not run (corpus returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\"), so no module/CVE range comparison is cited and the verdict rests solely on source + graph evidence. Severity HIGH rather than CRITICAL because it still requires a deployment that uses an OpenSSH-format EC/Ed25519 public key as the verification key."
          },
