          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Audience validation fails open on an absent `aud` claim. jwt._validate_aud (jwt.py:316) returns early at jwt.py:336 when `\"aud\" not in claims`, and the graph shows NO call of any kind between the function definition at line 316 and that check at 336 \u2014 i.e. there is no `if audience: raise JWTClaimsError(...)` guard. Consequently a caller that explicitly passes audience=\"my-client-id\" to jwt.decode still accepts a validly-signed token that carries no `aud` claim at all, because the enforcement branch at jwt.py:348 (`audience not in audience_claims`) is never reached. The complementary `require_aud` option defaults to False (jwt.py:114 `tmp0[\"require_aud\"] = False`), so nothing else closes the gap. A token minted by the same issuer for a different relying party, or a general-purpose token with no aud, is accepted as if it were audience-scoped \u2014 cross-service token replay.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived from source: _validate_aud returns early when the `aud` claim is absent, and the audience-presence guard is sitting right there COMMENTED OUT at jwt.py:337-338 (`# if audience: / #     raise JWTError('Audience claim expected, but not in claims')`). So a caller doing jwt.decode(token, key, audience=\"my-client-id\") accepts a validly-signed token carrying no `aud` claim, because the enforcement at jwt.py:348 (`audience not in audience_claims`) is unreachable past the line-339 return. I verified the whole chain myself: decode(..., audience=None) jwt.py:55 -> _validate_claims(claims, audience=audience, options=defaults) jwt.py:156-164 -> `if options.get(\"verify_aud\"): _validate_aud(claims, audience=audience)` jwt.py:481-482, with verify_aud defaulting True (jwt.py:116) but require_aud defaulting False (jwt.py:124) and _validate_claims:462-467 enforcing presence only for truthy require_* options. Graph agrees and shows the sink is genuinely reachable: scan-scoped query over jose/jwt.py lines 316-350 returns 29 call nodes whose first entry after the def at 316 is `\"aud\" not in claims` at 336 (nothing inspects `audience` in between; 337-338 are comments so emit no nodes), and line 336 plus the raises at 348/349 all carry reachable_from_entry=true. TWO CORRECTIONS to the analyst. (1) Location is 336-339, not 336-336. (2) The analyst's exploitation claim is overstated: \"a token minted by the same issuer for a different relying party ... is accepted\" is FALSE -- that token has an `aud` naming the other party and IS correctly rejected at line 348. Only tokens with NO `aud` claim at all fail open, so the attack needs the same signing key to also mint aud-less tokens (realistic: OAuth access tokens vs OIDC ID tokens off one key). Mitigating context the analyst omitted, which caps this at MEDIUM rather than HIGH: the behavior is deliberate and documented -- the decode docstring at jwt.py:63-65 says enforcement applies \"If the 'aud' claim is included in the claim set\", tests/test_jwt.py:349-353 (test_aud_empty_claim) explicitly asserts the permissive outcome, `require_aud` is the documented opt-in fix, and RFC 7519 4.1.3 mandates rejection only \"when this claim is present\". It remains a real fail-open insecure default, since PyJWT and the wider ecosystem raise MissingRequiredClaimError here, so a caller passing audience= reasonably expects enforcement and silently gets none. Version pinned exactly to python-jose 3.3.0 (jose/__init__.py:1); exploit_search returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\", so I have no exploit-reference evidence either way and assert neither exploitable-in-the-wild nor patched on that basis."
